  "notes": "Standard Deduction Worksheet for Dependents, line 3: the basic 2025 standard deduction for the filing status ($15,750 single/MFS, $31,500 MFJ/QSS, $23,625 HoH), reusing tax.us.2025.parameter.standard-deduction-base v1. v2: head of household and qualifying surviving spouse each require the filer's answer that the status's tests are met (tax.us.2025.hoh.tests-met, tax.us.2025.qss.tests-met), read only on that status; yes publishes the same lookup as v1, no refuses the line (DEPENDENCY_INVALID, naming the answer so the page shows its question beside the refusal) so the standard deduction, taxable income and the tax are not figured on that status's amounts, and an unanswered answer blocks naming it. This is the only rule that reads the standard deduction base, and line 12e reads the standard deduction on every route, so the standard deduction, Tax Table column and rate schedule either status carries are downstream of it. The preferential-rate ceilings themselves (Qualified Dividends and Capital Gain Tax Worksheet lines 6 and 13) read filing status directly and still show that status's ceiling after a no or no answer, but every worksheet line that combines a ceiling with taxable income (line 7 onward) is downstream of this rule and blocks, so no amount on the return uses them. Single, married filing jointly and married filing separately read nothing new."
